__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── Environment files — canonical block, KEEP LAST ───────────────────────────
# Ignore every `.env*` variant; track only templates.
#
# Why a glob instead of a list: the rules above enumerated specific names, so
# anything not on the list fell through — `.env.production`, `.env.staging`,
# `.env.vercel`, and ad-hoc copies like `.env.bak` / `.env.old` / `.env.save`
# were all committable. Non-template `.env` files have reached this estate's
# history before (BSU's "secret incident #27556716", crm7's `.env` carrying a
# service_role key, JWT secret and Postgres password). An enumerated list only
# ever covers the names someone thought of.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: git applies the LAST matching rule, so the negations must sit
# below the glob, and this block must stay at the end of the file. A later
# `.env*` line anywhere below would silently re-ignore the templates.
#
# Check a name before trusting it — grep of this file is not the authority:
#     git check-ignore -v --no-index .env.production
.env*
!.env*.example
!.env*.template
!.env*.sample

# ── Agent scratch — IGNORED HERE, DELETED BY scripts/reclaim-ephemera.mjs ──────
# Ignoring is only half of it. An ignored file is INVISIBLE, which is worse than
# a tracked one: nobody ever sees it again to remove it. Measured 2026-08-27:
# 4.2 GB of session scratchpads accumulating at ~600 MB/day, 222 MB of Playwright
# output, 36 MB of loose scratch — none of which .gitignore would ever reclaim.
# Run `node scripts/reclaim-ephemera.mjs --apply` to actually free the disk.
.vg-tmp/
audit-out-*/
output/playwright/
output/xfer/
.agent-drive/
*.tsv
*.tgz
*.har
*.jsonl
